          "english": "Students in university towns may also have access to another lever for involvement in accelerated math: math circles. In these groups, which came out of an Eastern European tradition of developing young talent, professors teach promising K-12 students advanced mathematics for several hours after school or on weekends. The Los Angeles Math Circle, held at the University of California, Los Angeles, began in 2007 with 20 students and now has more than 250. \"These math circles cost nothing, or they're very cheap for students to get involved in, but you have to know about them,\" said Rusczyk. \"Most people would love to get students from more underserved populations, but they just can't get them in the door. Part of it is communication; part of it is transportation.\"",
